$ pwd                        //shows where you are within the file-systems
$ tail                       //shows last 10 lines of a file, especially useful for looking up in logs
$ echo                       //prints a specific message
$ MYVAR="hello world"        //declare a variable
$ echo $MYVAR                //prints the value of MYVAR    
$ touch test                 //creates an empty text file called 'test'
$ wc                         //gives information about a text file such as line count, word count etc. eg. wc -l counts how many lines
$ alias                      //list alias
$ unalias ll                 //$ alias ll='ls -l'
note: variables and alias would disappear when system reboots, add it into $ vim .bashrc in home directory permanently
!! repeats the previous command. This is especially useful with sudo !! when you forget to run a command with elevated privileges.
eg. 
$ echo "Today is $(date)" > /home/student/today.txt
$ touch /home/student/file_$(date +%F)


3 ways to get help in Linux:
1. man <command>            //man means manual here
2. command --help
3. info command
note: in the exam, man doc and usr/share/doc are the two important doc locations

1. how to use man <command>
eg.
$ man ls
keyboard: 
up and down for line up and line down 
space and b for page up and page down 
/<key-word> for searching a key word; n jump to next key word, shift+n jump to last key word
g jump to top of the man page, shift+g jump to bottom of the man page
q for quit the man page

2. how to user command --help
first update man database: $ sudo mandb
second search a key word, eg. user: $ man -k user
note: there would be scenarios that one item correspondences to multiple man entries, eg. passwd
$ man 1 passwd        //look up 'passwd' in section 1
$ man 5 passwd        //look up 'passwd' in section 5


